SES $\to$ no result to track predictive intensity. \textit{Need to express it as Stoch.\ Process.}

$\to$ Multi-dimensional $P_t$.

Defining $\to$ statistical model has to specify all (and only) the assumptions, allowing to write the joint law of the stoch.\ process $(Y_t)$, i.e.\ write
\[ P(Y_1,\dots,Y_n) \]
for any $n$ and at all times $t_1,\dots,t_n$. \textit{Stoch.\ process.}

\textbf{Tuning} $\alpha$:
\begin{itemize} \item consider a grid of values $\alpha \in (0,1)$ \item for each $\alpha$, compute one-step-ahead forecasts for $t = 1,\dots,T$ \item ``score'' the forecasts; pick $\alpha$ giving best predictive performance \end{itemize}

Popular scores: MAE, MSE, MAPE (scale-free).

\textbf{Markov property:}
\[ p(y_t \mid y_0, y_1, \dots, y_{t-1}) = p(y_t \mid y_{t-1}),\quad \forall t \ge 1. \]

\textbf{Practically:} to assign the finite-dim.\ distributions of a Markov chain, enough to specify
\begin{itemize} \item the initial distribution $p_0(\cdot)$ of $Y_0$, \item the transition probabilities $p_{i,j}(t) = P(Y_t = j \mid Y_{t-1} = i),\ i, j \in \mathcal{Y}$. \end{itemize}

\textit{See: indep.\ of time!} $\to$ \textbf{Homogeneous Markov chain.}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{SSM EXAMPLE --- Random walk with drift (discretised motion law)}}

\textbf{\textcolor{red}{Setup.}}

$\bullet$ State equation: $\theta_t \;=\; \theta_{t-1} \;+\; v \;+\; w_t, \qquad w_t \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0, W),$ with $\theta_0 \sim \mathcal{N}(m_0, C_0)$.

$\bullet$ Observation equation: $Y_t \;=\; \theta_t + \varepsilon_t, \qquad \varepsilon_t \overset{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2).$

The state moves on average velocity of  $v$ per step, with a random shock $w_t$ at each step; observations are noisy snapshots of the (unobserved) position.




\textbf{Side-by-side.}
\begin{tabular}{l|l|l} & RW + drift & DLM local linear trend \\ \hline State dimension & $1$ ($\theta_t$ = position) & $2$ ($\mu_t$ = level, $\beta_t$ = slope) \\ Slope / drift & constant $v$ (fixed parameter) & $\beta_t$ (time-varying, random walk) \\ Adaptability & slope cannot change & slope drifts naturally \\ Captures regime shifts in slope? & no & yes \\ Number of noise sources & $1$ ($w_t$) & $2$ ($w_{1,t}, w_{2,t}$) \\ Recovers RW + drift when? & --- & $W_2 = 0$ and $\beta_0 = v$ \end{tabular}

\textbf{One-line intuition.}
\[ \text{RW + drift: \emph{constant} slope.} \qquad \text{DLM local linear trend: \emph{drifting} slope.} \]
RW + drift is the special case where the slope is locked in as a constant parameter; the local linear trend frees it to evolve. If your series has a slope that bends over time (e.g.\ growth accelerating then slowing), use the local linear trend; if the slope is genuinely steady, RW + drift is simpler and enough.
